# gate/crosspartition.subjects — the subject roster for gate/crosspartition.sh.
#
# IT IS A FILE AND NOT A LIST INSIDE THE SCRIPT, so that "zero subjects" is a
# state the gate can actually be in and can therefore be required to fail on
# (GAP-201). A gate whose subject count is a constant in its own source has not
# proven it would notice the count going to zero.
#
# One row per subject:
#
#     <shape>  <expected exit status>  <comma-separated `nm` pin list: a bare
#                                       name must be DEFINED in the linked
#                                       image, `!name` must be ABSENT from it>
#
# The expected status is the answer the ENTRY returns, and it is chosen so that
# only the reached partition can produce it. The pin list is the second pin: a
# correct answer with the reached relation absent from the image would mean the
# answer came from somewhere other than the partition under test.
#
# THE `!` PINS ARE WHAT MAKE THE `text` ROW MEAN ANYTHING. `text` requires
# `duo_str_to_i64` to be DEFINED, because its reached partition needs the string
# runtime and the entry does not — GAP-232, where the reached artifact's `need`
# was dropped and only the entry's reached the link line. A compiler that put all
# three bootstrap units on every link line would satisfy that row without ever
# reading a `need`, so every shape whose reached partition needs NO runtime
# requires the same symbol to be ABSENT.
#
# The roster is checked in both directions. A row naming a shape the gate
# cannot materialize fails; a shape the gate can materialize that no row names
# also fails. Deleting a line does not quietly drop a subject.
#
# AND THE THIRD COLUMN IS DERIVED, NOT DECLARED HERE. Which direction a row must
# pin `duo_str_to_i64` in, and which RELATION its reach pin may name, are both
# decided by the `materialize` arm that wrote the subject's sources — it converts
# text or it does not, and it declares the relation or it does not. The gate reads
# both off those sources, so a pin edited here to name something the arm never
# wrote is refused rather than silently satisfied. Editing a pin means editing the
# arm.
#
# AND THE DERIVATION RESTS ON THE ENTRY NEEDING NOTHING, which is now checked
# too. Every direction above is a claim about what the REACH needs; an arm whose
# ENTRY converts text puts `duo_str_to_i64` on the link line by itself, which
# satisfies the presence row without any reached need and makes the absence rows
# refuse a correct link line. No subject's entry may convert text.
#
# shape  answer  pins
duo      42      _helper__twice,!duo_str_to_i64
trio     44      _deeper__plus,!duo_str_to_i64
fan      25      _deeper__plus,!duo_str_to_i64
text     43      _helper__value,duo_str_to_i64
